    budHowtoTitle:'How to use the Budget Calculator', budHowtoDesc:'Step-by-step guide to budgeting',
    budHowtoRows: [['— HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR —','Create a personal budget in minutes'],['1. Enter income','Enter all your income — salary, rental income, benefits, etc. Use "+ Add" to add more items.'],['2. Enter expenses','Choose a category from the dropdown (Housing, Groceries, Transport, etc.), name the item, and enter the amount. Add as many as you want.'],['3. Click Calculate','You get an overview of monthly balance, savings rate, annual surplus, and a breakdown of expenses by category.'],['4. Download as CSV','Click "Download as CSV" to save your budget as a file you can open in Excel.'],['— HOW TO READ THE RESULTS —',''],['Monthly balance','Income minus expenses. Green = surplus, red = deficit.'],['Savings rate','What percentage of your income you save. 10-20% is recommended as a minimum.'],['Expenses by category','Shows where your money goes. Use the bar chart to find the largest expense items.'],['— GOOD TO KNOW —',''],['The 50/30/20 rule','A simple rule of thumb: 50% for needs (housing, food, transport), 30% for wants (entertainment, clothing), 20% for savings and debt repayment.'],['Adjust monthly','A budget is a living document. Review it each month and adjust based on actual figures.']],
    bilHowtoTitle:'How to use the Car Cost Calculator', bilHowtoDesc:'Step-by-step guide to car costs',
    bilHowtoRows: [['— HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR —','Calculate what it costs to own a car'],['1. Choose car brand','Select a car brand from the list. "Average" uses typical Norwegian figures from NAF and OFV.'],['2. Fill in details','Purchase price, ownership period, annual mileage, fuel type and insurance. The more accurate, the better the estimate.'],['3. Click Calculate','You get total ownership cost, cost per month and per kilometer.'],['— HOW TO READ THE RESULTS —',''],['Cost per month','Everything included: depreciation, fuel, insurance, service, tires, fees and tolls.'],['Cost per km','Useful for comparing with public transport or other alternatives.'],['Depreciation','By far the largest cost for most. New cars lose the most value in the first years.'],['— GOOD TO KNOW —',''],['EV vs. fossil car','EVs have lower fuel and fee costs, but often higher purchase price. Total cost can be similar over time.'],['Insurance varies greatly','Young driver in Oslo pays several times more than experienced driver in rural areas. Check multiple offers.']],
    npvHowtoTitle:'How to use the Profitability Analysis (NPV/IRR) calculator', npvHowtoDesc:'Step-by-step guide to NPV and IRR',
    npvHowtoRows: [['— STEP BY STEP —','How to do an investment analysis'],['1. Investment amount','Enter the total amount you invest today. E.g.: buying equipment, property, starting a project.'],['2. Discount rate','Use your required rate of return. Typically WACC (8-12%) or alternative return. Higher rate = stricter standard.'],['3. Cash flows','Enter expected net cash flow (revenue minus expenses) for each year. Use realistic estimates.'],['4. Press Calculate','The calculator gives you NPV, IRR, payback period and profitability index.'],['— HOW TO READ THE RESULTS —',''],['NPV (net present value)','Positive NPV = the investment returns more than required. The higher, the better.'],['IRR (internal rate of return)','The rate that makes NPV = 0. Compare with your required return. IRR > requirement = good investment.'],['Payback period','How many years to recover the investment. Shorter is better — but don\'t reject a good project just because it takes time.'],['Profitability Index (PI)','Ratio of present value of income to investment. PI > 1 = profitable. PI of 1.3 means 30% return above requirement.'],['— GLOSSARY —',''],['NPV (Net Present Value)','Present value of future cash flows minus investment'],['IRR (Internal Rate of Return)','The rate that gives NPV = 0'],['WACC','Weighted average cost of capital'],['— INTERPRETATION —',''],['NPV > 0','Profitable – accept investment'],['NPV < 0','Unprofitable – reject'],['IRR > WACC','Value-creating project'],['IRR < WACC','Value-destroying project'],['— REFERENCE —',''],['Discount rate','Usually WACC (pre-tax)'],['Typical WACC','8–12%'],['Payback period','Shorter is better'],['— EXAMPLE 1: NEW MACHINE —','Manufacturing company evaluates new machine'],['Investment','1,000,000 kr'],['Cash flow per year','300,000 kr for 5 years (increased production, lower maintenance)'],['Discount rate','10% (WACC)'],['NPV','137,236 kr → Profitable! The investment returns more than 10%.'],['— EXAMPLE 2: RENTAL PROPERTY —','Private person evaluates buying rental property'],['Investment','3,000,000 kr (equity + loan)'],['Net rental income per year','150,000 kr (after interest, maintenance, tax)'],['Expected sale gain year 5','500,000 kr'],['Discount rate','7% (alternative return from stock market)'],['Assessment','Compare NPV with alternative investment in funds — consider risk and liquidity.'],['— EXAMPLE 3: STARTUP —','Entrepreneur evaluates new business'],['Investment','500,000 kr'],['Year 1-2','Negative: -100,000 and -50,000 (startup)'],['Year 3-5','Positive: 200,000, 300,000, 400,000'],['Important','Use higher discount rate (15-20%) for startups because risk is higher.'],['— GOOD ADVICE —',''],['Be conservative with estimates','It is better to be positively surprised than to lose money. Use realistic (not optimistic) cash flows.'],['Use the right discount rate','For companies: WACC. For individuals: alternative return. For risky projects: add 3-5%.'],['Don\'t forget residual value','Does the project have value after year 5? Include sale value or scrap value in the last year\'s cash flow.'],['Think about tax','Cash flows should be after tax. Remember that depreciation provides tax benefits.'],['— COMMON MISTAKES —',''],['Mixing cash flow and profit','NPV uses cash flow (money in/out), NOT accounting profit. Depreciation is not cash flow.'],['Forgetting working capital','New business often needs money tied up in inventory and receivables — this is an investment.'],['Too low discount rate','Using 3% (bank rate) instead of 10% (WACC) makes everything look profitable. Use the market rate.'],['Ignoring opportunity cost','The money you invest could be placed elsewhere. NPV analysis accounts for this via the discount rate.'],['Only looking at payback period','Short payback is nice, but a project that takes longer can have much higher NPV. Look at the whole picture.']],
    npvInfoRows:[["— GLOSSARY —",""],["NPV (Net Present Value)","Present value of future cash flows minus investment"],["IRR (Internal Rate of Return)","The rate that gives NPV = 0"],["WACC","Weighted average cost of capital"],["— INTERPRETATION —",""],["NPV > 0","Profitable – accept investment"],["NPV < 0","Unprofitable – reject"],["IRR > WACC","Value-creating project"],["IRR < WACC","Value-destroying project"],["— REFERENCE —",""],["Discount rate","Usually WACC (pre-tax)"],["Typical WACC","8–12%"],["Payback period","Shorter is better"]],
    npvInfoTitle:"Interpretation guide",
    npvInfoDesc:"How to read NPV and IRR",
    npvExamplesTitle:"Real-world examples",
