    agaSectionInput: 'Salary and zone',
    agaSubtitle: 'Enter gross annual salary, choose AGA zone, holiday pay and OTP — and we\'ll calculate the full annual cost for the employer. Norwegian 2026 rates per zone (I, Ia/II, III, IVa, IV, V), 1G = 136 549 kr. OTP basis follows the Mandatory Occupational Pensions Act § 5-2 (between 1G and 12G). Everything happens locally in your browser.',
    agaTitle: 'Employer NI contribution',
    aksjerSubtitle: 'How shares work — how you make money, what the expected return and risk are, and how share tax and shielding deduction actually play out. Norwegian 2026 rates.',
    aksjerTitle: 'Shares',
    aksjerAAsk: 'On a Shares Savings Account (ASK), you can buy and sell shares and equity funds without paying tax on gains until you withdraw the money from the account. You can move freely between funds within ASK without a tax event. Tax comes on withdrawal, and the compound interest effect on the deferred amount can be significant over 15–20 years.',
    aksjerASkjerm: 'A small portion of share returns is tax-free each year — as compensation for the fact that you could have placed the money risk-free in a bank. The shielding rate for 2026 is 3.6% of your purchase price. If you hold shares worth 200,000 kr, the shielding deduction is around 7,200 kr — that portion of the year\'s return becomes tax-free.',
    aksjerAvkastningP1: 'Historically, the stock market has returned around 7% per year on average over long periods (Oslo Stock Exchange, global indices). But individual years vary enormously — +40% one year, −30% the next. The average only emerges over 10–20 years.',
    aksjerAvkastningP2: 'Example: 3,000 kr/month for 20 years = 720,000 kr invested. With 7% average return that becomes around 1.55 million kr. More than half the final amount is compound interest, not what you saved yourself.',
    aksjerBreadCurrent: 'Shares',
    aksjerBreadHome: 'Home',
    aksjerBreadPersonlig: 'Personal finance',
    aksjerCtaDesc: 'See what 7% average return does to monthly savings over 10–30 years',
    aksjerCtaTitle: 'Calculate your savings in the Savings Calculator',
    aksjerEyebrowAvkastning: 'The long view',
    aksjerEyebrowFeller: 'Common pitfalls',
    aksjerEyebrowInntekt: 'Income sources',
    aksjerEyebrowRisiko: 'Volatility',
    aksjerEyebrowSkatt: 'Tax 2026',
    aksjerEyebrowTips: 'Practical',
    aksjerFactboxFellerHdr: 'The pitfalls',
    aksjerFactboxInntektHdr: 'Two sources of return',
    aksjerFactboxKurs: '2. Capital gains — the share increases in value and you sell for more than you paid.',
    aksjerFactboxUtbytte: '1. Dividends — the company shares profits with owners (typically 1–3% per year of the share price).',
    aksjerFelle1: 'The hunt for the next Apple — professional managers rarely beat the index. The probability of an individual investor doing so is even lower.',
    aksjerFelle2: 'Panic-selling on a downturn — shares that fall usually rise again. Selling low = locking in the loss.',
    aksjerFelle3: 'Too little diversification — 1–5 shares = very high risk. 30+ shares or one broad index fund = much safer.',
    aksjerFootNote: '* Historical figures based on Oslo Stock Exchange / MSCI World. Actual return can be far lower or higher — shares are volatile and past performance does not guarantee future results. Tax rates and the shielding rate are updated annually by the Tax Administration.',
    aksjerFooterDisc: 'Indicative information. Not professional advice.',
    aksjerH2Avkastning: 'Expected return',
    aksjerH2Feller: 'Three pitfalls to avoid',
    aksjerH2Inntekt: 'How do you make money on shares?',
    aksjerH2Risiko: 'How large is the risk?',
    aksjerH2Skatt: 'Share tax and the adjustment factor',
    aksjerH2Tips: '5 tips that actually work',
    aksjerQAsk: 'Why do many defer tax using ASK?',
    aksjerQSkjerm: 'What is the shielding deduction?',
    aksjerRisikoP1: 'Shares are volatile — they fluctuate greatly in value and can fall 30–50% in a single year. The 2008 financial crisis gave around −50%, the Covid crash in March 2020 around −30% in a matter of weeks.',
    aksjerRisikoP2: 'Over 15–20 years, broadly diversified equity saving has almost always outperformed banks and bonds. But short-term investments (under 5 years) carry real risk — the probability of a loss in any given 5-year period is not negligible.',
    aksjerSkattP1: 'Individuals pay effectively 37.84% tax on share gains and dividends. The figure comes from the gain being adjusted by factor 1.72 before being taxed at the ordinary income rate (22%): 22% × 1.72 = 37.84%.',
    aksjerSkattP2: 'If you sell with 100,000 kr in gains, around 37,840 kr goes to tax. The shielding deduction (see below) reduces this slightly.',
    aksjerTip1: 'Spread the risk — buy an index fund, not individual shares. You then own hundreds of companies at once.',
    aksjerTip2: 'Invest regularly — monthly saving smooths out price fluctuations over time (no "timing" needed).',
    aksjerTip3: 'Long time horizon — at least 10 years, preferably 15+. Shorter = higher risk of being forced to sell at a bad time.',
    aksjerTip4: 'Low costs — index funds cost 0.2–0.4% annually, actively managed funds 1–2%. The difference erodes returns over time.',
    aksjerTip5: 'Use ASK — a Shares Savings Account (ASK) allows tax-free movement between funds within the account.',
