    kalkEnhetskostLblEkvtotCap: '(kr, entire production)',
    kalkEnhetskostResHeadline: 'Result',
    kalkEnhetskostPTotal: 'Total costs',
    kalkEnhetskostPAntall: 'Units produced',
    kalkEnhetskostPEnhet: 'Unit cost',
    kalkEnhetskostDivNote: 'Spreads all of the period\'s costs evenly across every unit produced.',
    kalkEnhetskostSumEkv: 'Total eq. units',
    kalkEnhetskostPKostEkv: 'Cost / eq. unit',
    kalkEnhetskostSelvPerVariant: 'Full cost per variant',
    kalkEnhetskostEkvNote: 'Equivalence numbers weight the cost — heavy variants carry more per unit.',
    kalkEnhetskostFormelToggle: 'Show formula',
    kalkEnhetskostF1: 'Simple division: Unit cost = Total costs / Units produced',
    kalkEnhetskostF2: 'Equivalent units = Σ (Quantity × Equivalence number)',
    kalkEnhetskostF3: 'Cost per equivalent unit = Total costs / Equivalent units',
    kalkEnhetskostF4: 'Full cost per variant = Cost per equivalent unit × Equivalence number',
    kalkEnhetskostFNote: '“Equivalence numbers weight the variants by how many resources they use — so a heavy variant carries more of the cost.”',
    kalkEnhetskostGuideKicker: 'How to calculate',
    kalkEnhetskostGuideTitle: 'Division when the units are alike — equivalence when they are not',
    kalkEnhetskostStep1: '<b>Simple division.</b> If you make one uniform product, you divide the period\'s total costs by the number of units produced. Done.',
    kalkEnhetskostStep2: '<b>Equivalence numbers.</b> If you have variants that use different amounts of resources, you give each one a number relative to the reference (= 1.00). A variant that costs 40 % more to make gets 1.40.',
    kalkEnhetskostStep3: '<b>Equivalent units.</b> Multiply quantity by the equivalence number and add it up. Divide the total cost by that sum — then give each variant its share according to its equivalence number.',
    kalkEnhetskostTableTitle: 'The example in table form',
    kalkEnhetskostTableIntro: '993 000 kr / 4 620 eq. units = 214.94 kr per equivalent unit. Each variant\'s full cost is that figure multiplied by its equivalence number.',
    kalkEnhetskostRowA: 'A (reference)',
    kalkEnhetskostRowSum: 'Total',
    kalkEnhetskostWhoKicker: 'Who uses it',
    kalkEnhetskostWhoTitle: 'Three typical cases',
    kalkEnhetskostWho1Lbl: 'Mass production',
    kalkEnhetskostWho1Desc: 'One product in high volume — simple division gives the cost per unit directly.',
    kalkEnhetskostWho2Lbl: 'Product range',
    kalkEnhetskostWho2Desc: 'Same process, different sizes — equivalence numbers allocate the cost fairly.',
    kalkEnhetskostWho3Lbl: 'Process industry',
    kalkEnhetskostWho3Desc: 'Brewery, dairy, foundry — variants that share the same plant but make different demands on it.',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqQ1: 'What is an equivalence number?',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqA1: 'A ratio that shows how many resources a variant uses compared with the reference. The reference is set to 1.00 — a variant that requires twice as much gets 2.00.',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqQ2: 'When is simple division enough?',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqA2: 'When all the units are alike and use the same amount of resources. Then the equivalence number is 1.00 for everything, and division gives the same answer as the equivalence method.',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqQ3: 'How do I find the right equivalence numbers?',
    kalkEnhetskostFaqA3: 'Look at what drives the cost — machine time, material consumption or labour hours per unit. The ratio between the variants on the most important cost driver gives you the equivalence number.',
    kalkEnhetskostRel1Desc: 'Full costing from materials to price, with overhead add-ons and profit.',
    kalkEnhetskostRel2Desc: 'Set the right price from the cost per unit.',
    kalkEnhetskostRel3Desc: 'From the salary you want to what you actually need to charge per hour.',
