    kalkLandedcostResPrisEks: 'Suggested price excl. VAT',
    kalkLandedcostResPrisInkl: 'Suggested price incl. VAT',
    kalkLandedcostNoteWithMargin: 'The suggested price is calculated from landed cost and your target margin, excl. VAT. VAT is added for the end customer — rate 25 %.',
    kalkLandedcostNoteNoMargin: 'The calculation is done excl. VAT. Enter a target margin to get a suggested retail price.',
    kalkLandedcostFormelToggle: 'Show formula',
    kalkLandedcostF1: 'Freight per unit = Total freight / Number of units',
    kalkLandedcostF2: 'Customs duty per unit = Cost of goods × duty%',
    kalkLandedcostF3: 'Landed cost = Cost of goods + Freight/unit + Customs duty + Insurance + Fees/unit + Packaging',
    kalkLandedcostF4: 'Suggested price excl. VAT = Landed cost / (1 − margin)',
    kalkLandedcostFNote: '“Import VAT is normally neutral (input = output) for VAT-registered businesses, so it is kept outside the cost of the goods themselves.”',
    kalkLandedcostGuideKicker: 'How to calculate',
    kalkLandedcostGuideTitle: 'From cost of goods to landed cost',
    kalkLandedcostStep1: '<b>Spread the freight over the number of units.</b> Total freight divided by the quantity gives freight per unit — 15 000 kr over 500 units is 30 kr each.',
    kalkLandedcostStep2: '<b>Add customs duty, insurance and fees.</b> Duty and insurance are calculated on the cost of goods. The clearance fee is spread over the shipment the same way as the freight.',
    kalkLandedcostStep3: '<b>Set the suggested price from your margin.</b> Divide the landed cost by (1 − margin) for the price excl. VAT. Add VAT for the end customer.',
    kalkLandedcostTableTitle: 'The example step by step',
    kalkLandedcostTableNote: 'The item costs 100 kr from the supplier, but lands at 142 kr per unit once freight, duty, insurance and clearance are included. That is the figure to price from — not the invoice price.',
    kalkLandedcostWhoKicker: 'Who uses it',
    kalkLandedcostWhoTitle: 'Three typical cases',
    kalkLandedcostWho1Lbl: 'Importer · container load',
    kalkLandedcostWho1Desc: 'A large freight bill spread over many units gives a low freight share per item — but fees and duty still count.',
    kalkLandedcostWho2Lbl: 'Online shop · small shipments',
    kalkLandedcostWho2Desc: 'Few units per shipment means freight and clearance hit hard per item. Price accordingly.',
    kalkLandedcostWho3Lbl: 'Wholesaler · repricing',
    kalkLandedcostWho3Desc: 'Landed cost fed into the margin calculation gives a suggested retail price that actually holds up.',
    kalkLandedcostFaqQ1: 'What is landed cost?',
    kalkLandedcostFaqA1: 'What the item actually costs you by the time it is in stock — cost of goods plus freight, customs duty, insurance, clearance fees and packaging, allocated per unit. This is the figure the price should be built on, not the supplier invoice alone.',
    kalkLandedcostFaqQ2: 'Should VAT be included?',
    kalkLandedcostFaqA2: 'Import VAT is normally neutral (input = output) for VAT-registered businesses, so it is kept outside the cost of the goods themselves. The calculation is done excl. VAT; VAT is only added in the retail price to the end customer.',
    kalkLandedcostFaqQ3: 'How do I allocate freight and fees?',
    kalkLandedcostFaqA3: 'The simplest method is to divide the total by the number of units in the shipment. If your goods differ widely in value or volume, you can allocate by value or weight instead — but per unit is good enough for most.',
    kalkLandedcostRel1Desc: 'Full costing from materials to price, with overhead add-ons and profit.',
    kalkLandedcostRel2Desc: 'Set the right retail price from the cost per unit.',
    kalkLandedcostRel3Desc: 'Cost per unit — even when the variants differ in how demanding they are.',
    kalkNetthandelTitle: 'E-commerce and unit economics',
    kalkNetthandelSubtitle: 'Do you make money on every order — and what can you afford to pay for a customer?',
