    agaHowtoTitle: 'How to use the employer NI contribution (AGA) calculator',
    agaHowtoLead: 'Employer national insurance contribution (arbeidsgiveravgift) is what the employer pays the state on top of the salary. It is an extra cost for the employer — it is not deducted from the employee\'s pay.',
    agaHowtoP1: '<strong>Gross salary per year</strong> is the agreed annual salary before tax. <strong>Holiday pay</strong> is added because it too counts as pay subject to the contribution.',
    agaHowtoP2: '<strong>Zone</strong> determines the rate, and follows the municipality where the business is registered — not where the employee lives. Zone I is 14.1 %, zone V is 0 %.',
    agaHowtoP3: '<strong>OTP rate</strong> is the mandatory occupational pension the employer saves for the employee. The minimum is 2 % of salary from the first krone up to 12G. The OTP contribution is subject to employer NI as well.',
    agaHowtoP4: 'The total at the bottom is what the employee actually costs the employer: salary + holiday pay + employer NI contribution + OTP.',
    vatHowtoTitle: 'How to use the VAT calculator',
    vatHowtoLead: 'The calculator works both ways — from the price excluding VAT to the price including it, and back. Choose the rate first; the field label then changes to match what you actually enter.',
    vatHowtoP1: '<strong>25 %</strong> is the standard rate. <strong>15 %</strong> applies to foodstuffs (food and drink), <strong>12 %</strong> to passenger transport, hotels and cinema, and <strong>0 %</strong> is zero-rated — for example books and exports.',
    vatHowtoP2: 'To find the VAT inside a price that already includes it, you do not divide by 1.25 — you multiply by 0.2. That is a common mistake, and the calculator gets it right both ways.',
    vatHowtoP3: '<strong>The adjustment calculator</strong> further down is something else: it is used when the use of a capital asset (building, machine) changes after the deduction has been taken, so that part of the input VAT must be reversed.',
    reiseHowtoTitle: 'How to use the travel deduction calculator',
    reiseHowtoLead: 'The travel deduction (reisefradrag, also called the commuter deduction) covers travel between your home and your fixed place of work. It is based on distance — not on what the journey actually cost you.',
    reiseHowtoP1: '<strong>One-way distance</strong> is multiplied by 2 and by the number of working days. The rate is 1.90 kr per kilometre.',
    reiseHowtoP2: '<strong>Working days per year</strong>: 230 is the norm (52 weeks minus five weeks of holiday and public holidays). If you work from home part of the week, you have to lower the number.',
    reiseHowtoP3: '<strong>Tolls and ferry</strong> per day are added to the travel cost, but only if you actually pay them daily — and you must be able to document it.',
    reiseHowtoP4: 'A fixed threshold of 12 000 kr is subtracted from the total. Only what remains gives a deduction, and the deduction basis is capped at 120 000 kr. The tax saving is 22 % of the net deduction.',
